---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import PhoneMockup from "../../components/PhoneMockup.astro";
import CaptionedFigure from "../../components/CaptionedFigure.astro";
import BlockQuote from "../../components/BlockQuote.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import CaseStudyCardGrid from "../../components/ux/CaseStudyCardGrid.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/ux/CaseStudyCard.astro";
import CaseStudySectionHeading from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySectionHeading.astro";
import CaseStudyBeforeAfter from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyBeforeAfter.astro";
import CaseStudyProsConsCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProsConsCard.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import {
  BooksIcon,
  SnakeArrowIcon,
  XYZIcon,
  TrackpadClickIcon,
  DangerPopupIcon,
  AdjustmentIcon,
  PhoneIcon,
} from "../../utils/icons";
import {
  blankIphoneMockup as iPhone,
  kingdraMobileView as MobileView,
  flow1,
  flow2,
  flow3,
  flow4,
  menubar1,
  iconbar,
  hamburgerBefore,
  hamburgerAfter,
  horizontalSlotsBefore as horizontalBefore,
  horizontalSlotsAfter as horizontalAfter,
  marginsBefore,
  marginsAfter,
  modalBefore,
  modalAfter,
  pwaBefore,
  pwaAfter,
  tabsBefore,
  tabsAfter,
  uxBanner as UXBanner,
} from "../../utils/images";

const sidebarItems = [
  { text: "Overview", href: "#overview" },
  { text: "Context", href: "#context" },
  { text: "Roadblocks", href: "#roadblocks" },
  { text: "Considerations", href: "#considerations" },
  { text: "Navigation", href: "#navigation" },
  { text: "Adaptations", href: "#adaptations" },
  { text: "Flow", href: "#flow" },
  { text: "Retrospective", href: "#retrospective" },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="The Journey to Mobile"
  subtitle="Kingdra Pokemon Teambuilder - October 2024"
  pageTitle="The Journey to Mobile: Kingdra Pokemon Teambuilder - Liam Sullivan"
  sidebarItems={sidebarItems}
  projectRole={["Software Engineering", "Product Design"]}
  team={["Solo"]}
  tools={["Figma", "Photoshop"]}
  timeline={["1 Month"]}
  githubLink="https://github.com/liambsulliva/pokemon-teambuilder"
  demoLink="https://pokedraft.liambsullivan.com"
  sidebarClassName="fixed right-[calc((100vw-1150px)/2-7rem)] top-56"
  isCaseStudy={true}
>
  <Image
    src={UXBanner}
    alt="Kingdra Teambuilder"
    class="mx-auto max-h-[700px] w-auto"
    loading="eager"
    slot="hero"
  />

  <section class="overview-section" id="overview">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Overview"
      title="Rebuilding Kingdra for phones first"
    >
      <p>
        Following the creation of my Pokemon Teambuilder in June, users testing
        the web app on their phone ran into a significantly higher rate of bugs
        and grievances compared to their peers on desktop platforms. Mobile was
        an afterthought during the initial development process.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tasked myself with rebuilding the UI for mobile devices, reducing the
        cost of interaction and easing the cognitive load for users that want to
        use the application on the go.
      </p>
      <p>
        These improvements reduced the overall scrolling time between UI
        elements and significantly reduced time to select Pokémon for a given
        team.
      </p>
    </CaseStudySectionHeading>
  </section>

  <section id="context">
    <div class="split-context">
      <div class="split-context__main">
        <CaseStudySectionHeading
          eyebrow="Context"
          title="Desktop-first development left mobile users stranded"
        />
        <p>
          In June of 2024, I started development for this Pokémon Teambuilder
          project as a way to learn <a
            href="https://nextjs.org/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">Next.js</a
          > and build my first real full-stack application. The project was a lot
          of trial and error, and I spent a ton of time linking up database schemas,
          calling APIs, and writing the core logic.
        </p>
        <p>
          All of which allowing the Teambuilder to
          <a
            href="https://bulbapedia.bulbagarden.net/wiki/Stat#Determination_of_stats"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">mimic the math</a
          > that the core Pokémon games use to calculate stats. After deploying the
          app, I enlisted the help of my Pokémon fan friends to test the app. One
          problem was clear immediately.
        </p>
        <BlockQuote>
          <h2 class="!m-0 text-white">
            The mobile experience was absolutely horrendous.
          </h2>
        </BlockQuote>

        <p>
          They initially tested the app on their phones, because why wouldn't
          they? Mobile users make up <a
            href="https://explodingtopics.com/blog/mobile-internet-traffic#mobile-internet-top-stats"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">over 60% of global internet traffic</a
          >. Many of them were running errands when I posted the question in the
          Discord, and thought they could check it out really quick on their
          break.
        </p>
        <p>
          The interface forced them to scroll for entire seconds to find the
          Pokémon they were looking to find, and the infinite scrolling feature
          significantly favored desktop users, who could leverage the width of
          the screen to quickly scan for the Pokémon they wanted.
        </p>
        <p>
          The message was loud and clear. The UI needed to be rebuilt with
          portable devices in mind.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-context__aside">
        <CaptionedFigure
          caption="The initial mobile UI. Look at how long it takes to scroll!"
        >
          <PhoneMockup contentImage={MobileView} phoneImage={iPhone} />
        </CaptionedFigure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="roadblocks">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Roadblocks"
      title="Libraries, S-curves, and missing depth"
    />
    <p>There were a few issues right out the gate...</p>
    <CaseStudyCardGrid>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Component Libraries"
        icon={BooksIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          Using the <a
            href="https://flowbite.com/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">Flowbite</a
          > component library saved initial development time but locked me into an
          inflexible ecosystem. The cost of switching was too high, so I had to work
          within their framework for the redesign.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="The S-Curve Design"
        icon={SnakeArrowIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          The S-Curve UI design worked well for desktop users but proved
          problematic on screens with limited horizontal space, making it a poor
          choice for mobile devices.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Lack of Dimensionality"
        icon={XYZIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          Web apps lack native gesture support without heavy JavaScript use,
          making it challenging to implement the stacked UI elements and
          navigation that mobile apps typically use.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
    </CaseStudyCardGrid>
    <Callout label="THE GOAL?">
      Take a dense UI, even by desktop standards, and make it feel at home on
      smaller devices.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="considerations">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Considerations"
      title="Hover, feedback, and hierarchy on a small screen"
    />
    <p>
      Interacting with the web is radically different on mobile devices.
    </p>
    <CaseStudyCardGrid variant="row">
      <CaseStudyCard
        variant="row"
        title="Hover State Alternatives"
        icon={TrackpadClickIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          Hover states don't work on touch devices, requiring alternative ways
          to show interactivity. I looked into implementing explicit visual
          indicators to replace hover previews, like using a tappable
          information button for speed stat calculations instead of hovering
          over the stat itself. This ensured mobile users could access the same
          information as desktop users through more touch-friendly interactions.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        variant="row"
        title="Interactive Feedback"
        icon={DangerPopupIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          While desktop interfaces rely on cursor feedback and hover states,
          touch interfaces needed a different approach. I explored options
          ensuring all elements remained within comfortable thumb reach for
          right-handed users, like placing all Pokemon on the team in a
          scrolling vertical list.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        variant="row"
        title="Information Hierarchy"
        icon={AdjustmentIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          The desktop's expansive layout had to be rethought for mobile to
          prevent cognitive overload. I planned to prioritize information
          through modals and strategic button placement, with special attention
          to the Pokemon selection interface where quick filtering through
          dropdowns became essential.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
    </CaseStudyCardGrid>
  </section>

  <section id="navigation">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Navigation"
      title="Two prototype directions that never fully landed"
    />
    <p>
      After brainstorming a bit, I started prototyping some layouts, neither of
      which I fully loved.
    </p>
    <div class="prototype-option-grid">
      <CaseStudyProsConsCard
        title="Bottom Tab Bar"
        image={menubar1}
        imageAlt="Bottom Tab Bar"
        pros={[
          "Easily accessible for one-handed use, familiar to iOS users, clean and minimalist glassy interface",
        ]}
        cons={[
          "Can't see added Pokémon without switching tabs, not enough features to justify multiple tabs",
        ]}
      />
      <CaseStudyProsConsCard
        title="Icons Up Top"
        image={iconbar}
        imageAlt="Icons along the top navigation"
        pros={[
          "Keeps bottom navigation clear for content and eliminates need for sidebars or nested menus",
        ]}
        cons={[
          "Hard to reach with one hand, confusing to new users who may not know what the icons do",
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="adaptations">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Adaptations"
      title="Concrete UI moves that reduced scroll and friction"
    />
    <p>
      After settling on a layout that worked, I immediately jumped into Tailwind
      to make some adjustments.
    </p>
    <CaseStudyCardGrid>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Hamburger Menu"
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={hamburgerBefore}
          after={hamburgerAfter}
          beforeAlt="Navigation before hamburger menu"
          afterAlt="Navigation after hamburger menu"
        />
        <p>
          Replaced the fixed infinite scrolling navigation with a collapsible
          hamburger menu to save valuable screen space. This mobile pattern is
          familiar, while maintaining full functionality.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>

      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Margins"
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={marginsBefore}
          after={marginsAfter}
          beforeAlt="UI before margin adjustments"
          afterAlt="UI after margin adjustments"
        />
        <p>
          Optimized margins for mobile screens while preserving touch targets
          and readability. This allowed for more content to be visible at once,
          especially within modal interfaces.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>

      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Convert Tabs to Dropdowns"
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={tabsBefore}
          after={tabsAfter}
          beforeAlt="Navigation before tab conversion"
          afterAlt="Navigation after tab conversion"
        />
        <p>
          Transformed the horizontal tab system into dropdown menus to prevent
          line breaks and maintain filtering options in a mobile-friendly
          format, which was particularly useful for the Pokemon selection
          interface.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>

      <CaseStudyCard
        title="PWA Migration"
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={pwaBefore}
          after={pwaAfter}
          beforeAlt="App before PWA implementation"
          afterAlt="App after PWA implementation"
        />
        <p>
          Enhanced the mobile experience through PWA implementation, enabling
          app installation, faster loading times through caching, and improved
          performance with reduced server-side operations.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>

      <CaseStudyCard
        title={`"Add Pokemon" Button + Modal`}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={modalBefore}
          after={modalAfter}
          beforeAlt="UI before modal implementation"
          afterAlt="UI after modal implementation"
        />
        <p>
          Replaced the spread-out Pokemon selection interface with a focused
          full-screen modal, accessible via a single "Add Pokemon" button.
          Features touch-optimized controls for filtering and searching.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>

      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Horizontal Pokemon Slots"
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <CaseStudyBeforeAfter
          slot="lead"
          before={horizontalBefore}
          after={horizontalAfter}
          beforeAlt="Pokemon slots before horizontal layout"
          afterAlt="Pokemon slots after horizontal layout"
        />
        <p>
          Converted the grid layout to a horizontal list, making team management
          more intuitive on mobile and increasing the Pokemon editor's touch
          target. Provides a natural scanning experience from top to bottom.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
    </CaseStudyCardGrid>
  </section>

  <section id="flow">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Flow"
      title="How the rebuilt mobile experience comes together"
    />
    <p>So how does this all come together?</p>
    <div class="flow-step-grid">
      <figure>
        <Image src={flow1} alt="Flow Image 1" />
        <figcaption>
          A vertical landing page layout lets users easily browse their Pokemon
          team. Teams can be imported from or exported to <a
            href="https://play.pokemonshowdown.com/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">Pokemon Showdown</a
          >.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure>
        <Image src={flow2} alt="Flow Image 2" />
        <figcaption>
          The hamburger menu provides quick access to game modes, team options,
          and account settings. The login button transforms into a user icon
          when signed in.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure>
        <Image src={flow3} alt="Flow Image 3" />
        <figcaption>
          Pokemon details appear in a space-efficient modal. Competitive mode
          shows stats and moves, while casual mode displays encounter locations
          and alternate forms.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure>
        <Image src={flow4} alt="Flow Image 4" />
        <figcaption>
          The "Add Pokemon" button opens a full-screen modal with filtering
          options by tier, generation, and name search. Multiple Pokemon can be
          added in succession.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="retrospective">
    <CaseStudySectionHeading
      eyebrow="Retrospective"
      title="Lessons from converting a desktop tool to mobile"
    />
    <p>
      Converting the Kingdra Teambuilder to mobile revealed valuable lessons
      about responsive design. What began as a simple port evolved into an
      exploration of mobile UX patterns and the complexities of adapting desktop
      functionality to smaller screens.
    </p>
    <CaseStudyCardGrid class="kingdra-retrospective-card-grid">
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="The Component Library Dilemma"
        icon={BooksIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          Flowbite's rigid structure, while perfect for desktop, required
          creative CSS solutions to adapt to mobile. In the future, I will build
          out components from scratch to give me a greater degree of control.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="Rethinking Touch Navigation"
        icon={TrackpadClickIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          Replacing the desktop sidebar with a hamburger menu proved to be more
          than just a space-saving measure. The new navigation system made for a
          much more intuitive mobile experience.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
      <CaseStudyCard
        title="The PWA Game-Changer"
        icon={PhoneIcon}
        titleClass="text-xl font-medium text-white"
      >
        <p>
          The Progressive Web App implementation brought a lot of benefits.
          Caching brought significant improvements in load times and performance
          that didn't exist in the original web implementation.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyCard>
    </CaseStudyCardGrid>
    <p>
      Going forward, the type coverage module needs to be re-implemented. In the
      current horizontal list implementation, displaying it on mobile is simply
      not feasible yet. A gesture-based system could make this desktop-optimized
      tool more accessible on mobile devices. Enhanced filtering options by
      types, stats, and moves would improve the user experience, especially on
      mobile and tablets where screen space is limited and only two columns of
      Pokemon can be shown at one time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Future development will focus on replacing Flowbite components with
      custom, device-agnostic solutions to improve flexibility and
      maintainability. By removing these dependencies, I'll have much more
      freedom in creating custom layouts.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style is:global>
  .kingdra-retrospective-card-grid .case-study-card__icon-wrap {
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
    min-height: 7.5rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .kingdra-retrospective-card-grid .case-study-card__icon-svg {
    width: 7rem;
    height: 7rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
